A real Banach space $X$ is said to admit a complex structure if there exists a bounded linear operator $J: X \to X$ such that $J^2 = -I_X$, where $I_X$ denotes the identity operator on $X$. A hyperplane of $X$ is any closed linear subspace of codimension one. Two Banach spaces are isomorphic if there exists a bounded linear bijection between them with bounded inverse.

Does there exist a real Banach space $X$ with the following three properties simultaneously?
(i) $X$ is not isomorphic to its hyperplanes;
(ii) $X$ admits a complex structure;
(iii) every hyperplane of $X$ also admits a complex structure.

